    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d785">

                <pb facs="0795" n="683" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg683"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/5259</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">General Tasker H. Bliss of the Commission to Negotiate
                        Peace to the Russian Ambassador to the United States</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bakhmeteff</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">temporarily at
                        Paris</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-07-04"><hi rendition="#italic">July 4,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for Monsieur
                        Bakhmeteff</hi>:</p>
                <p>With reference to the conversation between General Bliss and Mr. Bakhmeteff of
                    July 3rd, information is requested as to (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) the
                        <pb facs="0796" n="684" xml:id="pg684"/> measures the representatives of
                    Admiral Koltchak at Paris would propose in order to finance, organize and
                    support the anti-Bolshevist forces in the Baltic Provinces and Lithuania, and
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) the assurances they would give as to the
                    future autonomy of Esthonia, Latvia and Lithuania, together with a specific
                    statement as to the nature of the autonomy.</p>
                <p>The approaching withdrawal of the German forces and the critical situation in
                    these regions call for a firm military and political policy in order to maintain
                    this anti-Bolshevist front and not to jeopardize the gains that have already
                    been made from the Bolshevists. In view of the urgency of the matter an early
                    reply would be appreciated.</p>
                <p>Furthermore, the military situation requiring the immediate support and
                    strengthening of the Russian Volunteer anti-Bolshevist forces in the Baltic
                    Provinces, will General Rodzianko and Prince Lieven be recognized by the
                    representatives of Admiral Koltchak at Paris as the respective leaders of these
                    forces?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tasker H. Bliss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
